-- allium: 3
--
-- Biscuit Games — appearance
--
-- Scope
--   How every Biscuit Games surface looks: the three preferences that decide
--   it, how each negotiates with a preference the operating system has
--   already expressed, and the legibility the result has to reach.
--
-- Includes
--   - Theme, high contrast and animations, as the platform's shared
--     appearance settings.
--   - Theme starting dark, with system as the choice that follows the device
--     as it changes; animations deferring to a reduced-motion preference;
--     and high contrast turning on for a device that asks for more of it.
--   - The legibility every combination of theme and high contrast has to
--     reach, stated as ratios rather than as colours, and the one case that
--     is exempt from it.
--
-- Excludes
--   - The controls that change these settings. A settings panel belongs to
--     the product that owns it: this module states what the settings mean and
--     what the result owes the reader, never where the switches live, how
--     they are guarded, or how they are stored.
--   - Colour values, palettes, markup and animation mechanics. Naming a floor
--     the colours have to clear is not the same as choosing them: this module
--     says how far apart two things must be, never what either one is.
--     src/app.css is what answers it.
--   - Anything a particular game does with the result. A game states its own
--     states and any separation those states need between them.
--
-- Dependencies
--   None. This is the platform's root module, and it imports nothing so that
--   a game can inherit the surface below rather than restate it.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Given
------------------------------------------------------------

given {
    -- Preferences the reader expressed to their operating system rather than
    -- to Biscuit Games. Biscuit Games reads them and never writes them.
    --
    -- They are stated one by one rather than gathered behind an entity because
    -- this module imports nothing: a root module has no governing
    -- specification for an external entity to come from, and three named
    -- booleans say where they come from just as well.
    prefers_dark_colour_scheme: Boolean
    prefers_reduced_motion: Boolean

    -- The reader asked their operating system for more contrast everywhere,
    -- not Biscuit Games in particular. It is a separate question from the
    -- colour scheme: a device can ask for more contrast in either one.
    prefers_more_contrast: Boolean
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- system means "whatever the device asks for, as it changes". light and dark
-- each hold, whatever the device asks for; dark is where Biscuit Games starts.
enum ThemeChoice { system | light | dark }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

entity AppearanceSettings {
    theme: ThemeChoice
    high_contrast: Boolean
    animations: Boolean

    -- High contrast as it actually applies, which is what anything rendering
    -- should read. high_contrast above is the reader's own answer and stays
    -- exactly as they left it; a device asking for more contrast turns the
    -- palette on without overwriting that answer.
    high_contrast_active: high_contrast or prefers_more_contrast
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- Contrast ratios, as WCAG 2.2 computes them, for the guarantee that names
    -- them. They are thresholds a rendering either meets or does not, and they
    -- name no colour. These are the two AA bars: one for text, one for
    -- everything that is not text.
    minimum_text_contrast: Decimal = 4.5
    minimum_boundary_contrast: Decimal = 3.0
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Defaults
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One appearance record, existing from the first visit. Theme starts dark, not
-- at system: near-black is the platform's identity (docs/design/direction.md,
-- "Dark is home"), so a first visit lands on it whatever the device prefers.
-- Light, and system's following of the device, stay one choice away.
default AppearanceSettings appearance_settings = {
    theme: dark,
    high_contrast: false,
    animations: true
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- How Biscuit Games looks, decided jointly by the reader's settings and the
-- device. This surface applies to the hub and to every game wearing the
-- design system.
surface Appearance {
    let settings = appearance_settings
    let dark_active =
        if settings.theme = system: prefers_dark_colour_scheme
        else: settings.theme = dark
    let animations_active = settings.animations and not prefers_reduced_motion

    exposes:
        dark_active
        animations_active
        settings.high_contrast_active

    @guarantee SystemFollowsTheDeviceAsItChanges
        -- While theme is system, Biscuit Games matches the device's colour
        -- scheme and keeps matching it as it changes. Light and dark — the
        -- default included — hold on their own, and the device is not
        -- consulted.

    @guarantee ReducedMotionOverridesTheAnimationSetting
        -- Animations run only when the animations setting is on and the device
        -- expresses no reduced-motion preference. The device preference wins: a
        -- reader who asked their system for less motion gets none from Biscuit
        -- Games, whatever the setting says.

    @guarantee MoreContrastFromTheDeviceTurnsHighContrastOn
        -- High contrast applies when the setting is on or the device asks for
        -- more contrast. The device wins the same way it does for motion: a
        -- reader who asked their system for more contrast gets it without
        -- having to find the setting and ask a second time.

    @guarantee AppearanceNeverCarriesMeaningAlone
        -- Neither the theme nor high contrast is what makes anything readable.
        -- Nothing on any surface is distinguished by colour alone in any
        -- combination of these settings: every state colour helps to show also
        -- carries a shape, a word, or both. A game that adds states of its own
        -- inherits this obligation and states how it meets them.

    @guarantee EveryCombinationMeetsTheLegibilityFloor
        -- The floor holds in all four combinations of theme and high contrast,
        -- not only the one a change happened to be looked at in. Text on any
        -- control the reader can operate reaches config.minimum_text_contrast
        -- against what is behind it, and so does body copy; every such control
        -- is identifiable as one — by a boundary reaching
        -- config.minimum_boundary_contrast against the page where it draws a
        -- boundary, and by its own words where it draws none, since a control
        -- with no drawn edge is found by reading it and an edge that is not
        -- there cannot be measured. High contrast raises the floor nowhere: it
        -- is a second palette that has to clear the same bar, not the version
        -- where legibility is finally attended to. A game that puts two of its
        -- own states side by side owes a separation between them as well and
        -- states that figure itself; this module states the bars every surface
        -- owes the page.

    @guarantee AnUnavailableControlIsExempt
        -- A control the reader cannot operate is held to none of those
        -- figures, exactly as WCAG 2.2 exempts an inactive component from
        -- 1.4.3 and 1.4.11 — and so is the text that names it, because a label
        -- that stayed bright beside a control that had gone quiet would
        -- disagree with it about whether the thing can be used. Dimming is how
        -- unavailability reads, and a dim that had to clear the bar a live
        -- control clears would not read as one.
        -- What the exemption does not buy is anything about how that state is
        -- known. It is spent only while the control genuinely cannot be
        -- operated, never on one that is merely quiet or incidental; the
        -- unavailability is carried in the accessibility tree, so a screen
        -- reader is told in words what the eye reads as dim; and a dimmed
        -- control keeps every non-colour indication its live form carried,
        -- because AppearanceNeverCarriesMeaningAlone admits no exemption at
        -- all.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- That the device wins is settled, and matches how reduced motion already
-- behaves. What is not settled is whether it is the last word: a reader who
-- asked their system for more contrast but wants this palette left alone has
-- nowhere to say so, because the setting they would reach for is the one the
-- device is already overriding.
open question "Contrast escape hatch - may a reader turn high contrast off while the device asks for more?"
